Summary: Alan Mathison Turing (23 June 1912 – 7 June 1954) was an English mathematician, logician, cryptanalyst, philosopher, and pioneering computer scientist. He is most famous for formalising the concepts of algorithm and computation with the Turing machine, a theoretical construct that underlies modern computer architecture and the field of theoretical computer science, earning him the title "father of computer science." During World War II, Turing worked at Bletchley Park, where he led Hut 8 in breaking the German Enigma cipher, providing critical intelligence that helped the Allies win the war. His work laid the foundations for modern digital computers, artificial intelligence, and computational theory. After the war, he contributed to early computer design, proposed the Turing test for machine intelligence, and advanced mathematical biology. Turing's legacy also includes his tragic personal story: he was prosecuted for homosexuality in 1952, leading to his chemical castration and early death, which has since become a symbol of the injustice faced by LGBTQ+ individuals. His posthumous pardon in 2013 and the continued celebration of his contributions underscore his profound impact on technology, science, and civil rights.
